.TH CHTTPSVR_REGISTER_STREAMING_HANDLER 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler \- register a streaming handler on the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler(chttpsvr srv,
                                  chttp_method_t method,
                                  const char *pattern,
                                  chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Like chttpsvr_register_handler, but the route is marked as a
streaming route, which makes chttpsvr_req_read available to the
handler so that it can read the body one piece at a time.  On a route
that is not a streaming route chttpsvr_req_read returns -1.

A streaming handler reads its body at the pace of the client and can
keep its thread for as long as the client takes.  So it runs on a pool
of its own with chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_thread_count threads, and
never on the reactor thread or on the worker pool of the buffered
routes, which means slow streaming clients can take up at most that
pool.  The server creates the pool when the first streaming request
arrives.  Its threads count as workers of the server, so a call that
a handler must not make on its own server, such as chttpsvr_destroy()
or a restart, is refused or fatal there exactly as in a buffered
handler.  If chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_thread_count is
CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF, streaming handlers run on the worker
pool instead; chttpsvr_start(3) describes what that changes.

CHTTP_ANY as method matches every HTTP method on the pattern; see
chttpsvr_register_handler for the full rules of CHTTP_ANY.

The server routes the request as soon as the headers are parsed,
before it reads a single body byte, and the worker then reads the
body by itself, batch by batch, straight from the socket.  The
reactor thread never reads it.  chttpsvr_req_read hands each batch to
the handler as it arrives.  Once the body has ended it returns 0, and
every later call returns 0 as well.  It returns -1 for a dropped
connection, for a body above max_body_size and for a client that
stalled.  chttpsvr_config_t.stream_read_timeout_us bounds each gap and
chttpsvr_config_t.max_body_read_duration_us, when it is set, bounds
the whole read; chttpsvr_req_stream_error tells these causes apart.
chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_bps also ends the read, with
ccol_timed_out, of a client whose average rate falls below that floor.

A streaming request that finds every streaming thread busy waits in a
queue of chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_queue_capacity entries, and it
holds no thread while it waits.  When that queue is full, or when a
request has waited there longer than
chttpsvr_config_t.streaming_queue_timeout_us, the server answers at
once with 503 Service Unavailable and "Retry-After: 5", and the
handler never runs for that request.  None of this blocks the reactor
thread.

The pattern rules of chttpsvr_register_handler apply here too:
pattern must start with '/', a pattern with two consecutive slashes or
a trailing slash is rejected, a {name} segment must contain only
[A-Za-z0-9_], and the same {name} must not appear more than once in
one pattern.  chttpsvr_register_handler has the full details of the
pattern checks and of how a path segment is decoded.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_NULL or a stale handle that
                  was destroyed; when method is neither one of the
                  seven methods nor CHTTP_ANY; when pattern or fn is
                  NULL; when pattern does not start with '/', has two
                  consecutive slashes or has a trailing slash; when a
                  segment of the form {name} contains a character
                  outside [A-Za-z0-9_]; or when the same {name}
                  appears more than once.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_read (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_context (3),
.BR CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT (3)
